WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.960
Let's talk about logistic regression, which is probably the single most widely used classification

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:07.460
algorithm in the world.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:10.860
This is something that I use all the time in my work.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:16.480
Let's continue with the example of classifying whether a tumor is malignant.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:21.180
Whereas before, we're going to use the label 1 or yes, the positive class to represent

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:28.180
malignant tumors and 0 or no negative examples to represent benign tumors.

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:34.780
Let's look at the data set where the horizontal axis is the tumor size and the vertical axis

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:41.060
takes on only values of 0 and 1 because it's a classification problem.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:46.620
You saw in the last video that linear regression is not a good algorithm for this problem.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:53.540
In contrast, what logistic regression will end up doing is fit a curve that looks like

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:59.260
this, a sort of S-shaped curve to this data set.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:05.540
And so for this example, if a patient comes in with a tumor of this size, which I'm showing

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:13.600
on the x-axis, then the algorithm will output 0.7, suggesting that it's closer or maybe

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:17.460
more likely to be malignant and benign.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:24.600
We'll say more later what 0.7 actually means in this context, but the output label y is

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.420
never 0.7 is only ever 0 or 1.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:34.380
To build up to the logistic regression algorithm, there's an important mathematical function

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:41.020
I like to describe, which is called the sigmoid function, sometimes also referred to as the

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:43.600
logistic function.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.940
The sigmoid function looks like this.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:52.100
Notice that the x-axis of the graphs on the left and right are different.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:58.940
In the graph to the left, on the x-axis is the tumor size, so it's all positive numbers.

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:06.040
Whereas in the graph on the right, you have 0 down here, and the horizontal axis takes

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:12.480
on both negative and positive values, and I've labeled the horizontal axis z.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:18.680
And I'm showing here just a range of negative 3 to plus 3.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:25.640
So the sigmoid function outputs values between 0 and 1, and if I use g of z to denote this

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:34.420
function, then the formula of g of z is equal to 1 over 1 plus e to the negative z, where

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:41.320
here e is a mathematical constant that takes on a value of about 2.7, and so e to the

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:47.260
negative z is that mathematical constant to the power of negative z.

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:55.400
Notice if z were really big, say 100, e to the negative z is e to the negative 100, which

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.260
is a tiny, tiny number.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:05.800
So this ends up being 1 over 1 plus a tiny little number, and so the denominator will

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:13.040
be basically very, very close to 1, which is why when z is large, g of z, that is the

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:18.360
sigmoid function of z, is going to be very close to 1.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:26.080
And conversely, you can also check for yourself that when z is a very large negative number,

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:35.540
then g of z becomes 1 over a giant number, which is why g of z is very close to 0.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:42.220
So that's why the sigmoid function has this shape where it starts very close to 0 and slowly

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:47.140
builds up or grows to the value of 1.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:54.820
Also in the sigmoid function, when z is equal to 0, then e to the negative z is e to the

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:57.740
negative 0, which is equal to 1.

00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:05.880
And so g of z is equal to 1 over 1 plus 1, which is 0.5.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:11.460
So that's why it passes the vertical axis at 0.5.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:16.140
Now let's use this to build up to the logistic regression algorithm.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.920
We're going to do this in two steps.

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:24.660
In the first step, I hope you remember that a straight line function like a linear regression

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:31.840
function can be defined as w dot product of x plus b.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:37.820
So let's store this value in a variable, which I'm going to call z.

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:42.060
And this will turn out to be the same z as the one you saw on the previous slide, but

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.100
we'll get to that in a minute.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:51.500
The next step then is to take this value of z and pass it to the sigmoid function,

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:55.580
also called the logistic function g.

00:04:55.580 --> 00:05:03.740
So now g of z then outputs a value computed by this formula, 1 over 1 plus e to the negative

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:04.740
z.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:08.840
That's going to be between 0 and 1.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:14.160
When you take these two equations and put them together, they then give you the logistic

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:28.080
regression model f of x, which is equal to g of w x plus b, or equivalently g of z, which

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:32.780
is equal to this formula over here.

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:36.700
So this is the logistic regression model.

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:42.620
And what it does is it inputs a feature or set of features x, and it outputs a number

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:45.440
between 0 and 1.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:51.440
Next let's take a look at how to interpret the output of logistic regression.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.360
We'll return to the tumor classification example.

00:05:55.360 --> 00:06:00.760
The way I encourage you to think of logistic regression as output is to think of it as

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:08.000
outputting the probability that the class or the label y will be equal to 1 given a

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.840
certain input x.

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:18.940
So for example, in this application where x is the tumor size and y is either 0 or 1,

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:24.720
if you have a patient come in and she has a tumor of a certain size x, and if based

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:32.940
on this input x, the model outputs 0.7, then what that means is that the model is predicting

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:39.060
or the model thinks there's a 70% chance that the true label y will be equal to

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.320
1 for this patient.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:47.860
In other words, the model is telling us that it thinks the patient has a 70% chance of their

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:51.440
tumor turning out to be malignant.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:54.200
Now let me ask you a question.

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:56.640
See if you can get this right.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:01.240
We know that y has to be either 0 or 1.

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:08.900
So if y has a 70% chance of being 1, what is the chance that it is 0?

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:15.600
So y has got to be either 0 or 1, and thus the probability of it being 0 or 1, these

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:20.320
two numbers have to add up to 1 or to 100% chance.

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:27.000
So that's why if the chance of y being 1 is 0.7 or 70% chance, then the chance of it

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:32.640
being 0 has got to be 0.3 or 30% chance.

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:38.120
If someday you read research papers or blog posts about logistic regression, sometimes you

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:47.780
see this notation that f of x is equal to p of y equals 1, given the input features x

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:51.920
and with parameters w and b.

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:58.640
What the semicolon here is used to denote is just that w and b are parameters that affect

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:04.500
this computation of what is the probability of y being equal to 1, given the input feature

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:06.000
x.

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:09.800
For the purpose of this class, don't worry too much about what this vertical line and what

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:12.160
the semicolon mean.

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:17.080
You don't need to remember or follow any of this mathematical notation for this class.

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:21.320
I'm mentioning this only because you may see this in other places.

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:26.140
In the optional lab that follows this video, you also get to see how the sigmoid function

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:28.640
is implemented in code.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:34.100
You can see a plot that uses the sigmoid function so as to do better on the classification

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:37.460
task that you saw in the previous optional lab.

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:41.660
Remember that the code will be provided to you, so you just have to run it.

00:08:41.660 --> 00:08:45.820
I hope you take a look and get familiar with the code.

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:47.980
So congrats on getting here.

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:53.580
You now know what is the logistic regression model as well as the mathematical formula

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:56.500
that defines logistic regression.

00:08:56.500 --> 00:09:02.020
For a long time, a lot of internet advertising was actually driven by basically a slight

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:04.780
variation of logistic regression.

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:08.680
This was very lucrative for some large companies and this was basically the algorithm that

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:13.260
decided what ad was shown to you and many others on some large websites.

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:17.580
Now, there's even more to learn about this algorithm.

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:22.580
In the next video, we'll take a look at the details of logistic regression.

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:28.800
We'll look at some visualizations and also examine something called the decision boundary.

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:33.880
This will give you a few different ways to map the numbers that this model outputs, such

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:42.640
as 0.3 or 0.7 or 0.65 to a prediction of whether y is actually 0 or 1.

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:47.320
So let's go on to the next video to learn more about logistic regression.
